    "turn_text": "I was well prepared for the sense that I'd been a private sector executive and then that alone would have been a tough transition, but then I'd have been in Congress 10 years. So the combination of the two, the executive and then the legislative branches, I think, prepared me well for it. Obviously, you never anticipate what the next crisis will be. So we have the three largest wildfires in this super estate. We had a global pandemic. But beyond that, you have to make sure those things never prevent you from doing the other things you want to do to move move your state forward. And who is the governor that you learn the most from? I'm like being in Congress where you are around everybody in a very social environment. Being governor is a lot more solitary. Yes, we get together once a year, maybe twice a year, whether it's Mark Gordon, Wyoming, Spencer Cox in Utah, or Michelle Long Christian in New Mexico. Those are some of the folks I've probably talked to the most. What have you found to be the most effective way to get a compromise? yeah it's always challenging as a as a governor you don't get the right the law you rely on a legend your legislature and actually one of the most common topics with governors get together as they like to complain about their legislatures it doesn't matter they could be our republic governors of the public and legislatures democratic over his democratic legislatures like all you know i can't get this is so They challenge us. There's things that we agree to that we wouldn't necessarily do on our own. And then we try to ultimately get them to do things that we think are kind of that big picture good for our states. I don't know if you were the first",
